/**
@page theory Theory

We describe here the theory for the abrupt interface AdResS version for which examples are
available in the release version of `MRMD`. The smooth interpolation common to other (H-)AdResS
flavors is also already implemented in `MRMD` — see mrmd::weighting_function::Slab and
mrmd::weighting_function::Spherical — but is not yet available in test-covered example scripts.

@section theory_symbols Notation

| Symbol | Meaning |
| ------ | ------- |
| \f$x_i\f$, \f$x_j\f$ | positions of particles \f$i\f$ and \f$j\f$ along the \f$x\f$-axis |
| \f$r\f$ | absolute distance between two particles |
| \f$x_{\Delta\textrm{/TR}}\f$ | position of the interface between the \f$\Delta\f$ and the TR region |
| \f$v(r)\f$ | underlying pair potential, e.g. truncated and shifted Lennard-Jones |
| \f$r_{\textrm{cap}}\f$ | radius below which the force is capped |
| \f$r_{\textrm{cut}}\f$ | cutoff radius of the pair potential |
| \f$F_{\textrm{th}}\f$ | one-body thermodynamic force |
| \f$\rho\f$ | particle density profile |
| \f$c\f$ | user-defined convergence prefactor of the thermodynamic force iteration |

@section theory_adress Abrupt interface AdResS

At the heart of AdResS being applied to simulate an open atomistically resolved system in
exchange with a reservoir through its boundary is the thermodynamic consistency of said reservoir.

Such a reservoir can be realized in a rectangular simulation domain by the atomistically resolved
(AT) region being encapsuled to the left and right by buffer zones (\f$\Delta\f$ regions) within
which the particles are also interacting atomistically. Beyond the \f$\Delta\f$ regions, in the
tracer (TR) region, the interactions are then abruptly switched off, so that the interaction
potential can be written as

\f[
    U(r, x_i, x_j) = \begin{cases}
            V(r) &\textrm{, for } x_i, x_j \in [-x_{\Delta\textrm{/TR}}, x_{\Delta\textrm{/TR}}] \\
            0 &\textrm{, else } \\
            \end{cases}\textrm{,}
\f]

where \f$x_i\f$ and \f$x_j\f$ are the positions of two particles \f$i\f$ and \f$j\f$ in
\f$x\f$-direction and \f$r\f$ is the absolute distance in between them.

It is emphasized that this does not necessarily comprise a Hamiltonian AdResS scheme in the
classical sense due to the discontinuities of such a potential at
\f$\pm x_{\Delta\textrm{/TR}}\f$, but that the TR region anyways represents merely an algorithm to
provide the \f$\Delta\f$ regions with the necessary number of particles and fluctuations thereof
such that they, in turn, can provide the AT region with thermodynamically consistent boundary
conditions.

The coupling of an atomistically resolved region to an ideal gas of non-interacting tracers follows
Ref. [10.1063/1.5143268](https://doi.org/10.1063/1.5143268) and is implemented in
mrmd::action::LJ_IdealGas. @ref tut_08_LennardJones_ProductionTracer runs the resulting setup.

@section theory_capping Force-capped interaction potential

The pair potential itself is

\f[
    V(r) = \begin{cases}
            v(r_{\textrm{cap}}) - \frac{\partial v}{\partial r} \big|_{r_\textrm{cap}} r_{\textrm{cap}} + \frac{\partial v}{\partial r}\big|_{r_\textrm{cap}} r &\textrm{, for } r_{\textrm{cap}} \geq r \\
            v(r) &\textrm{, for } r_{\textrm{cap}} < r \leq r_{\textrm{cut}} \\
            0 &\textrm{, for } r_{\textrm{cut}} < r \\
            \end{cases}\textrm{,}
\f]

that is, a radial potential force-capped beneath a radius \f$r_{\textrm{cap}}\f$ with \f$v(r)\f$
being a suitable pair-wise potential such as the truncated and shifted Lennard-Jones potential.

Capping keeps the integration stable when particles that were inserted or that crossed the
interface end up overlapping. It is applied through mrmd::action::LennardJones and
mrmd::action::LJ_IdealGas, and demonstrated in @ref tut_04_LennardJones_IdealGas_LocalCap.

@section theory_thermoforce Thermodynamic force iteration

The change of resolution, be it abrupt or smooth, introduces numerical artifacts that can be
compensated by a one-body thermodynamic force \f$F_{\textrm{th}}\f$. This force is calculated
during the setup stage of AdResS simulations in an iterative procedure

\f[
    F_{\textrm{th}}^{k + 1}(x) = F_{\textrm{th}}^{k}(x) - c \nabla \rho^{k}(x) \textrm{,}
\f]

where each iteration \f$k\f$ comprises a short AdResS simulation with applied thermodynamic force
\f$F_{\textrm{th}}^{k}(x)\f$, which is incremented by the gradient of the density profile
\f$\nabla \rho^{k}(x)\f$ averaged over this simulation weighted by a user-defined convergence
prefactor \f$c\f$. The procedure is considered converged when the density profile is flat to within
a desired tolerance. With the converged thermodynamic force, the AdResS production run can be
started.

The iteration follows Ref. [10.1063/1.5031206](https://doi.org/10.1063/1.5031206) and is
implemented in mrmd::action::ThermodynamicForce.
@ref tut_07_LennardJones_ThermodynamicForce runs it from a zero initial force, and
@ref tut_07_LennardJones_ThermodynamicForce_InitialGuess starts from a precomputed guess to
shorten the convergence.

@section theory_references References

- Coupling of an atomistically resolved region to an ideal gas:
  [10.1063/1.5143268](https://doi.org/10.1063/1.5143268)
- Thermodynamic force for adaptive resolution simulations:
  [10.1063/1.5031206](https://doi.org/10.1063/1.5031206)
- The MRMD implementation and its validation:
  [arXiv:2607.24574](https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2607.24574)
*/
